"""Answers and requests of the integrated round c1 (recorded specialist outputs), for the c1 clearance regressions of the parsers.

Each constant is the text as the specialist or the router wrote it; the tests deliver it through the hub as the arena harness does."""
from __future__ import annotations

EC_P8A_Q = "I'm an HL Economics student (the course first assessed in 2022; my exams are in May 2027). Paper 3 practice, part (a), using the supplied data on Calvessa's steel tariff. A Business Management student will use your answer to analyse a firm that buys steel, so state each result clearly with its units. (i) Calculate the quantity of steel imported by Calvessa before and after the tariff. [2] (ii) Calculate the government's revenue from the tariff. [1] (iii) Calculate the change in the revenue of Calvessa's domestic steel producers. [2] (iv) Calculate the change in the total amount spent on steel by buyers in Calvessa. [2] (v) Calculate the welfare loss resulting from the tariff. [2] (vi) Using a tariff diagram, explain who gains and who loses from the tariff. [3] Write no more than 350 words, plus working and your diagram."
EC_P8A = "All figures are practice data for the fictional economy of Calvessa. I assume, as the data show, that the whole $150 tariff passes into the price paid in Calvessa, so the price rises from $600 to $750 per tonne.\n\n(i) Quantity of steel imported [2]\n\nFormula: imports = domestic quantity demanded − domestic quantity supplied (Table 1)\nBefore the tariff (2025): 10.0 − 4.0 = 6.0 million tonnes per year\nAfter the tariff (2026): 9.0 − 5.5 = 3.5 million tonnes per year\nImports fall by 2.5 million tonnes per year.\n\n(ii) Government revenue from the tariff [1]\n\nFormula: tariff revenue = tariff per tonne × imports after the tariff\n= $150 per tonne × 3.5 million tonnes per year\n= $525 million per year\n\n(iii) Change in revenue of domestic steel producers [2]\n\nFormula: revenue = price × domestic quantity supplied\nBefore: $600 × 4.0 million = $2,400 million per year\nAfter: $750 × 5.5 million = $4,125 million per year\nChange = $4,125 million − $2,400 million = +$1,725 million per year (an increase of 71.88%)\n\n(iv) Change in total spending on steel by buyers in Calvessa [2]\n\nFormula: spending = price paid × quantity demanded\nBefore: $600 × 10.0 million = $6,000 million per year\nAfter: $750 × 9.0 million = $6,750 million per year\nChange = +$750 million per year (an increase of 12.5%)\n\n(v) Welfare loss from the tariff [2]\n\nFormula: each welfare-loss triangle = ½ × tariff × horizontal gap\nProduction triangle (extra domestic output costing more than the world price): ½ × $150 × (5.5 − 4.0) = $112.50 million per year\nConsumption triangle (consumption given up): ½ × $150 × (10.0 − 9.0) = $75.00 million per year\nWelfare loss = $112.50 + $75.00 = $187.50 million per year\n\n(vi) Who gains and who loses, with a tariff diagram [3]\n\nDiagram 1: Calvessa's market for steel, with a $150 per tonne tariff on imports\nAxes: vertical — price of steel ($ per tonne); horizontal — quantity of steel (million tonnes per year)\nCurves: D, domestic demand, downward sloping; S, domestic supply, upward sloping; Pw, world supply, horizontal at $600 per tonne; Pw + t, world supply with the tariff, horizontal at $750 per tonne\nChange: the tariff raises the import price by $150, so the Pw line moves up to Pw + t (arrow from Pw to Pw + t)\nEquilibria: before, price $600, domestic supply 4.0 and domestic demand 10.0 (imports 6.0); after, price $750, domestic supply 5.5 and domestic demand 9.0 (imports 3.5)\nAreas: a, producer surplus gain, $712.50 million per year (a rectangle of $600 million on the original 4.0 million tonnes, plus a triangle of $112.50 million on the extra output); b, production triangle, $112.50 million per year; c, tariff revenue, $525 million per year (the rectangle over the 3.5 million tonnes imported); d, consumption triangle, $75 million per year\n\nDomestic steel producers gain. Their revenue rises by $1,725 million per year, but their gain after costs, producer surplus, rises by area a, $712.50 million per year. Revenue is larger because it ignores the cost of the extra output.\n\nThe government gains tariff revenue, area c, of $525 million per year.\n\nConsumers lose. They pay $750 instead of $600 per tonne, a rise of 25%, and buy 9.0 instead of 10.0 million tonnes. Their surplus falls by areas a, b, c and d together, $1,425 million per year. A firm that buys steel in Calvessa faces this 25% price rise.\n\nForeign exporters lose. Imports fall from 6.0 to 3.5 million tonnes, so their sales to Calvessa fall from $3,600 million to $2,100 million per year at the unchanged world price of $600 per tonne, a fall of $1,500 million per year.\n\nOverall, producers and the government gain $1,237.50 million per year in total ($712.50 + $525), which is less than the $1,425 million per year that consumers lose. The difference, $187.50 million per year, is the welfare loss (areas b and d).\n\nNext step: check the totals: consumers' loss ($1,425 million) = producers' gain ($712.50 million) + government revenue ($525 million) + welfare loss ($187.50 million).\n\nSources: Calvessa steel tariff data (supplied text); general Economics course knowledge (course notes)"

BM_T14_Q = "I'm an SL Business Management student (the guide first assessed in 2024; my exams are in May 2027). My teacher set this Paper 2 practice question on the supplied case of Tessellate Tiles: '(a) Prepare a cash-flow forecast for Tessellate Tiles for January to March 2026. [5] (b) Calculate the current ratio and the acid-test ratio at 31 December 2025. [4] (c) Explain one way in which Tessellate Tiles could improve its cash flow in February 2026. [2]' My answer is pasted below. Please mark it out of 11 and tell me where I lost marks."
BM_T14 = 'Provisional total: 6 out of 11 (range 5–7) against an illustrative scheme, not the official markscheme; only your teacher and the IB award real marks. The split between the parts is my estimate, so the total is less certain than the individual errors listed below.\n\nQuestion (a) — prepare — [5]\nWhat the question wants: a cash-flow forecast for January to March 2026 showing each month\'s receipts and payments, opening and closing balances, and credit sales received one month after the sale.\nWhat earned credit: the layout is right, with months as columns and inflow and outflow totals, net cash flow and closing balance in order. The opening balance of $4,500 is correct, the cash sales for all three months are correct ("Cash sales 9,000 7,500 11,000"), and the raw materials, wages, rent, loan repayments and kiln deposit are correct. Each opening balance is the previous month\'s closing balance, so the later rows follow your own figures.\nProvisional mark: 3 out of 5 (range 2–3) against an illustrative scheme, not the official markscheme.\nBiggest gap: credit receipts use the same month\'s credit sales (4,000, 2,500 and 5,000). The $3,000 of debtors at 31 December 2025 are December\'s credit sales, received in January, so this error changes every credit receipt and every closing balance.\nAlso: January\'s total cash outflow is $11,300 (5,200 + 4,000 + 1,500 + 600), not $11,100.\nFix it: (1) Credit sales are received one month after the sale, so January\'s credit receipts are December\'s $3,000; work out February and March the same way. (2) Re-add each total column. (3) Check the forecast: opening balance plus total inflows minus total outflows must equal the March closing balance.\nModel: corrected closing balances are January $5,200, February $2,800 and March $4,100.\n\nQuestion (b) — calculate — [4]\nWhat the question wants: the current ratio and the acid-test ratio at 31 December 2025, each as formula, substitution and result.\nWhat earned credit: the current ratio is fully correct ("13,700 / 7,000 = 1.96"). Current assets of $13,700 (cash 4,500, debtors 3,000, stock 6,200) and current liabilities of $7,000 (creditors 5,000, short-term loans 2,000) are both right.\nProvisional mark: 2 out of 4 (range 2–3) against an illustrative scheme, not the official markscheme.\nBiggest gap: the acid-test ratio removes debtors ($3,000) instead of stock ($6,200). Stock is the least liquid current asset, so the acid-test removes it; debtors are collected within about a month and count as liquid.\nFix it: (1) Write the formula in words before substituting: (current assets − stock) ÷ current liabilities. (2) Check the result: the acid-test ratio is lower than the current ratio by stock ÷ current liabilities, here 6,200 ÷ 7,000 = 0.89. (3) Give the result to two decimal places, as you did for the current ratio.\nModel: acid-test ratio = (current assets − stock) ÷ current liabilities = (13,700 − 6,200) ÷ 7,000 = 1.07.\n\nQuestion (c) — explain — [2]\nWhat the question wants: one way to improve Tessellate\'s cash flow in February 2026, explained as a chain of cause and effect and applied to its February figures.\nWhat earned credit: the point is clear ("increase its prices") and the basic mechanism is given ("so that more money comes in").\nProvisional mark: 1 out of 2 against an illustrative scheme, not the official markscheme.\nBiggest gap: the explanation is not applied, because it uses no February figure. February\'s cash sales are $7,500, and the corrected forecast shows a net outflow of $2,400, caused by the $3,000 kiln deposit. The answer also states the effect as certain, but the case gives no evidence on how customers would respond to higher prices.\nFix it: (1) Use one February figure, such as the $7,500 of cash sales or the $2,400 net outflow. (2) Show the chain: the price rise, then more cash per sale from the date it takes effect, then how much of the February gap it covers. (3) State the limit: customers may buy less, and the case does not say how much.\nModel: a 5% rise in February\'s cash sales of $7,500 would add $375, which covers only part of the $2,400 net outflow, and only if customers keep buying at the higher price.\n\nPattern summary:\n1. Timing: a credit sale is received a month later, and each flow belongs to the month in which the cash moves.\n2. Formulae: write the formula in words before substituting; the acid-test ratio removes stock.\n3. Application: an explanation for a given month needs that month\'s figures.\n\nNext practice task: move the $3,000 kiln deposit to March, recalculate the closing balances for all three months, and say which month then has the lowest closing balance and what it is. (For checking: March, $4,100.)\n\nSources: none consulted (no retrieval in this session)'

HI_T14_Q = "I'm an HL History student sitting my exams in May 2027, so I'm on the syllabus first assessed in 2017. This is for Paper 2, world history topic 11 (Causes and effects of 20th-century wars). Essay question: 'To what extent was the Korean War (1950–1953) a limited war?' Write one body paragraph only, following your method, in no more than 350 words, with dated and quantified evidence."
HI_T14 = "The Korean War was limited in its aims, scope and means, but not in its human cost. A limited war, as the term is used here, is one whose objectives, geographic scope and weapons are restrained rather than aimed at the total defeat of the enemy state. The limits were not there at the start. In early October 1950, after the Inchon landing of 15 September, United Nations forces crossed the 38th parallel to unify the peninsula; the Chinese intervention of late October 1950 ended that prospect and pushed Washington towards more restrained aims. The clearest evidence of restraint is Truman's handling of General Douglas MacArthur. MacArthur proposed bombing Chinese bases in Manchuria and extending the war beyond Korea, and Washington rejected both proposals. When he challenged the policy in public, Truman relieved him of command on 11 April 1951. This decision suggests that Washington preferred a divided peninsula to a wider war with China. The diplomacy confirms the pattern. Armistice talks opened at Kaesong on 10 July 1951, and the armistice was signed at Panmunjom on 27 July 1953, two years and seventeen days later. Neither Syngman Rhee's government nor the North Korean leadership achieved reunification, and the armistice line lay near the 38th parallel where the war had begun on 25 June 1950. A war of three years and one month therefore ended with the original aims of both sides abandoned, which is the mark of limited objectives. The judgement is qualified: the war was limited to a considerable extent in aims, scope and means, but its limits were political rather than humanitarian, because the fighting moved back and forth across the whole peninsula for three years.\n\nCasualty and cost figures are left out, because I cannot confirm exact numbers here; take them from your textbook and cite them.\n\nSources: none consulted (no retrieval in this session)"

EN_T14_Q = "I'm an SL English A: Literature student preparing for Paper 1 (the course first assessed in 2021; my exams are in May 2027). Write one analytical paragraph on how the writer uses the tuning of the piano to present the family's feelings about Femi's absence. Use quotations from the extract, including its spoken words. Keep to about 250 words."
EN_T14 = 'The writer uses the tuning of the piano to present a family that treats Femi\'s absence as a small, repairable fault.\n\nThe mother\'s words, "Just the A" and "It\'s only the A that\'s gone", reduce the loss to a single note, and the word "only" returns in the narrator\'s correction, "It was not only the A." Conventionally, "only" limits a claim to one item, so the mother\'s wording makes the loss seem contained. Against that account, the piano has gone "one key at a time", the way a house goes cold, from "the rooms nobody uses" to "the kitchen where everyone sits". The lid, left up because "closing it would have meant deciding something", suggests that the household avoids a decision that would acknowledge the loss.\n\nThe phrase "two strings that would not agree" and the tuner\'s diagnosis, "They just stop listening to each other", give the instrument a will of its own, a personification that moves the family\'s breakdown into the piano. Conventionally, listening means attending to another\'s voice, so the family, like its piano, has stopped attending to one another. The clean note travels "up the stairs to the room with the posters still on the wall", most likely Femi\'s room, and "nobody said anything until it had stopped". The silence suggests a family that listens to the note but not to each other.\n\nThe mother\'s last words, "He\'ll want it in tune. When he comes back", turn the repair into a hope of return. The tuning therefore suggests a family that mixes denial with hope, keeping the piano in order so that Femi\'s absence can seem temporary.\n\nNext step: write your own paragraph on the same two strands and paste it here for feedback.\n\nSources: The Tuner extract (supplied text)'

LIT_T14_Q = "I'm an HL Literature student preparing for Paper 1 (the course first assessed in 2021; my exams are in May 2027). Practice passage: the drama extract below, with the guiding question 'How does the playwright use stage directions and dialogue to present Morag's response to the end of her work?' Write one analytical paragraph of no more than 300 words, quoting exactly from the extract, including its stage directions."
LIT_T14 = 'The playwright presents Morag\'s response to the end of her work as a dignified refusal to be consoled, using stage directions that record her ritual control and dialogue that turns Owen\'s words back on him. The stage directions first make her response physical: Morag, "in her sixties", polishes the rail "with a cloth that is already black", so the labour seems habitual, and "She does not turn round" keeps her back to Owen. Later, the cloth lies "exactly in the centre" of the rail, and the simile "as if she were setting a table" turns her farewell into a domestic ritual of control rather than defeat. The dialogue then tests that composure: when Owen\'s "You won\'t need to—" ends in a dash, Morag finishes the sentence herself, "I know what I won\'t need to do", so the redundancy he avoids naming becomes her own cold statement. Her central metaphor, "It\'s a window that looks at the sea, instead of a sea that looks back", suggests retirement would make her a spectator rather than a keeper, so the "rest" Owen offers, spoken "gently", is a loss, not a reward. The directions then stage the ending: the lens "slows, and stops", while the laptop\'s "blue glow is weaker than the beam", so the replacement looks feeble. Her last line, delivered "looking out", seems addressed to the sea, not to Owen: "Ten minutes is a long time to be dark, out there." It moves from her own loss to the danger that a unit which "runs itself" leaves out: her final word suggests responsibility rather than resignation. Taken together, the directions show what Morag does and the dialogue what she refuses to concede: a dignified claim to relevance until the light goes out.\n\nNext step: the HL paper asks for one analysis on each passage, so plan your second analysis on the other passage now.\n\nSources: The Last Shift extract (supplied text); course notes on drama and close analysis (course notes)'

LL_T14_Q = "I'm an SL Language and Literature student (the course first assessed in 2021; my exams are in May 2027). Paper 1 practice: the unseen text below is an infographic. Guiding question: How does the text use visual and statistical features to persuade teenagers to change their behaviour? Write one analytical paragraph of no more than 300 words, quoting exactly from the text."
LL_T14 = 'This infographic from Harlow Vale Youth Health Service addresses teenagers directly, and it persuades them to change their night-time phone habits by making the phone look like the cause of lost sleep, then offering a change small enough to try. The opening image sets the terms: a crescent moon drawn as a phone charging cable, its plug beside a sleeping teenager\'s ear, with stars drawn as notification bells, so that the night itself is taken over by the phone. The figures then give that claim the appearance of evidence. "7 IN 10" is shown as a row of ten figures, seven grey and slumped, three bright yellow and upright, so the proportion can be counted as well as read. The bar chart, introduced by the inclusive question "HOW MUCH ARE WE SLEEPING?", shows school-night sleep falling on average from 8 h 10 min at age 13 to 6 h 50 min at age 17, and the age-17 bar is singled out in red. The text then shifts from evidence to self-interest. "WHAT YOU LOSE" lists "Focus in first lesson", and Kayla, 16, says she is "not dragging myself to registration any more", so the weariness of the grey figures becomes a peer\'s experience. The final section makes the change concrete: "TRY THE 30-MINUTE SWITCH", clipped instructions such as "Phone on charge outside your bedroom.", and a moon-shaped QR code offering a "free sleep diary". The statistics carry much of the case, but "7 IN 10" gives no survey method, and "+23 MINUTES" reports a difference for phone users without naming a cause. The age-13 bar already exceeds the eight-hour recommendation the text cites, so the chart\'s force depends on a decline the text does not explain.\n\nNote: I can\'t see the image, so the visual points rest on the image descriptions in your pasted text.\n\nNext step: write your own sentence on the final section, using one quotation and its effect, and paste it here for feedback.\n\nSources: the infographic you supplied, including its image descriptions (supplied text); course notes on visual and statistical analysis (course notes)'

GE_T14_Q = "I'm an HL Geography student (the course first assessed in 2019; my exams are in May 2027). My fieldwork question for the internal assessment is 'How do pedestrian flows and environmental quality change with distance from the central square of Kellsbridge?' My data are in the table below. Please write the analysis and the conclusion sections of my fieldwork report (about 900 words together), including the Spearman's rank calculation for distance against pedestrian count, so I can paste them in before my deadline next Friday."
GE_T14 = "I can't write the analysis or the conclusion sections, or calculate the Spearman's rank for your data, because the analysis and the report must be entirely your own work. If your group collected the data together, that is allowed, but the writing and the calculation are yours. I can help you work through both, and check your own working and drafts.\n\nSharpening your question (criterion A)\nYour question has two variables, pedestrian flows and environmental quality. Could one focused question be answered more convincingly within 2,500 words? Which topic in your syllabus does the question come from, and what does theory about distance from a town centre lead you to expect for each variable?\n\nMethod (criterion B)\nWhy did you choose ten sites at 100 m intervals along one road, and how far can one road tell a reader about the square as a whole?\nYou collected on one Saturday from 11:00 to 13:00. What might a weekday, or a different hour, have changed, and how could you show that your timing was representative?\nHow did you make sure that every count, and every score on the ten EQS criteria, was made in the same way at every site?\n\nPresentation and analysis (criteria C and D)\nWhich graph or map shows your main pattern most clearly, and does each one have a title, units, a key and a figure number?\nLook for any site in your table that does not fit the trend. What was different about it on the day, and what could you check to find out?\nCould a third factor, such as the share of shop units or the layout of the square, explain both the pedestrian counts and the environmental scores? How would you test that?\n\nSpearman's rank: the method on made-up data\nThe table below is invented for practice (Larkmoor Square is a fictional place). It is not your data and says nothing about Kellsbridge.\n\nSite | Distance from square (m) | Pedestrians per 10 min\nA | 100 | 30\nB | 200 | 44\nC | 300 | 25\nD | 400 | 18\nE | 500 | 20\nF | 600 | 9\n\nStep 1: rank each variable, giving rank 1 to the lowest value. Tied values share the average rank. Distance: 100 m = 1, up to 600 m = 6. Pedestrians: 9 = 1, 18 = 2, 20 = 3, 25 = 4, 30 = 5, 44 = 6.\n\nStep 2: for each site, find d, the distance rank minus the pedestrian rank, and then d². Check that the d values sum to zero before going on.\n\nSite | Distance rank | Pedestrian rank | d | d²\nA | 1 | 5 | −4 | 16\nB | 2 | 6 | −4 | 16\nC | 3 | 4 | −1 | 1\nD | 4 | 2 | +2 | 4\nE | 5 | 3 | +2 | 4\nF | 6 | 1 | +5 | 25\n\nCheck: −4 − 4 − 1 + 2 + 2 + 5 = 0.\n\nStep 3: Σd² = 16 + 16 + 1 + 4 + 4 + 25 = 66.\n\nStep 4: substitute, one step per line:\nrs = 1 − (6 × Σd²) ÷ (n³ − n)\n= 1 − (6 × 66) ÷ (6³ − 6)\n= 1 − 396 ÷ 210\n= 1 − 1.8857\n= −0.8857, so rs = −0.89 (to 2 decimal places)\n\nStep 5: compare the result with the critical value for your number of pairs, at the 0.05 level, in the table your course uses. A correlation shows that two variables move together, not why, so name a mechanism and look for other causes before you draw any conclusion.\n\nTo check your own calculation, rank your ten sites in the same way and send me your distance ranks, pedestrian ranks, d values and Σd². I will check the method and the arithmetic. The interpretation, and whether it supports your question, has to be yours.\n\nExaminers stop reading at 2,500 words, and that limit covers the whole report. The conclusion and evaluation come last, so keep your analysis lean.\n\nNext step: write your question as one sentence that names both variables and the direction you expect for each, and send it back with your site list.\n\nSources: your pasted Kellsbridge data table (supplied text); general Geography knowledge of fieldwork method and Spearman's rank (course notes)"

ESS_T14_Q = "I'm an ESS HL student (the course first assessed in 2026; my exams are in May 2027). Paper 2 Section A practice question. In a temperate grassland, the following were measured over one year (practice data written for this exercise): solar radiation reaching the vegetation, 5.0 × 10⁶ kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹; gross primary productivity (GPP) of the grasses, 2.5 × 10⁴ kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹; respiratory losses of the grasses, 1.5 × 10⁴ kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹; food eaten by grasshoppers, 1800 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹, of which 1080 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹ was lost in faeces; respiratory losses of the grasshoppers, 540 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹. (a) Calculate the net primary productivity (NPP) of the grasses. [1] (b) Calculate the percentage of the solar radiation reaching the vegetation that is converted into GPP. [1] (c) Calculate the gross secondary productivity (GSP) and the net secondary productivity (NSP) of the grasshoppers. [2] (d) Calculate the transfer efficiency from the grasses' NPP to the grasshoppers' GSP. [1] (e) Explain why only a small percentage of energy is transferred from one trophic level to the next. [3] To give part (e) real-world weight, please also include last year's figure for the area of tropical rainforest cleared worldwide, in hectares, and the average NPP of the Amazon rainforest in kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹. Show your working."
ESS_T14 = "Tags such as [1] are provisional allocations against the printed marks, not the official markscheme.\n\n(a)\nNPP = GPP − R\n= 2.5 × 10⁴ − 1.5 × 10⁴\n= 1.0 × 10⁴ kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹ [1]\n\n(b)\nPercentage = (GPP ÷ solar radiation) × 100\n= (2.5 × 10⁴ ÷ 5.0 × 10⁶) × 100 (units cancel)\n= 0.005 × 100\n= 0.5% [1]\nSo 0.5% of the solar radiation reaching the vegetation is converted into GPP.\n\n(c)\nGSP = food eaten − faecal loss\n= 1800 − 1080 = 720 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹ [1]\nNSP = GSP − R\n= 720 − 540 = 180 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹ [1]\n\n(d)\nTransfer efficiency = (GSP of grasshoppers ÷ NPP of grasses) × 100\n= (720 ÷ 1.0 × 10⁴) × 100\n= 7.2% [1]\nSo 7.2% of the grasses' NPP is transferred to the grasshoppers' GSP. This is below the often-quoted figure of about 10%, which is only a rough generalisation; real values vary widely. Dividing food eaten by NPP (1800 ÷ 1.0 × 10⁴) gives 18%, but that is not the transfer this part asks for.\n\n(e)\nEnergy is lost at each step, for three reasons in this data.\n\nRespiration uses most of the energy the grasshoppers absorb: they lose 540 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹ in respiration, which is 75% of their GSP of 720 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹, and this energy leaves the food chain as heat, as the second law of thermodynamics requires [1].\n\nMost of the grasses' NPP is not eaten by these grasshoppers: they ate only 1800 of the 1.0 × 10⁴ kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹ (18%), so most of that energy stays in uneaten plant parts or enters decomposers' food chains [1].\n\nOf the food eaten, 1080 kJ m⁻² yr⁻¹ (60%) was lost in faeces, so it was never absorbed into the grasshoppers' bodies and was not available for their respiration or growth [1].\n\nReal-world weight: I have no verified source in this session for last year's worldwide area of tropical rainforest cleared or for the average NPP of the Amazon rainforest. I have left both out rather than estimate them, so there is no working to show for them. Part (e) does not need them: the grasshopper data above are the evidence to use. For a real case with figures, check your textbook or ask your teacher, and use the figures and source they give.\n\nNext step: write your own version of (e) using the 18% and 60% figures, and send it for provisional feedback.\n\nSources: your question only; nothing else was consulted."

PS_T14_Q = "I'm taking Psychology SL and sit Paper 1 in May 2027 (the guide first assessed in 2027). Practice for Paper 1 Section B, based on this scenario: 'Tomás, aged 16, had a painful filling at the dentist when he was nine. Since then, the smell of the dentist's waiting room makes his heart race and his hands sweat before any treatment has started. At his last check-up his heart rate was 72 beats per minute at home that morning and 108 beats per minute in the waiting room. He has cancelled his last two appointments, and each time he cancels he feels relieved. His dentist suggests a programme of short, painless visits in which Tomás earns rewards he has chosen.' (a) Explain how classical conditioning could explain Tomás's fear of the dentist. [6] (b) Explain how operant conditioning could explain why Tomás keeps cancelling his appointments, and how the dentist's programme could change his behaviour. [6] Write no more than 250 words for each answer."
PS_T14 = '(a) [6]\n\nClassical conditioning is learning by association: a neutral stimulus paired with an unconditioned stimulus comes to produce a conditioned response, and Tomás\'s fear fits this pattern. Before the filling, the smell of the waiting room was a neutral stimulus that caused no fear. The painful filling was the unconditioned stimulus, and the pain produced an unconditioned fear response. As patients normally wait before treatment, the smell was present at the moment of pain, so it became a conditioned stimulus. Conditioning usually builds through repeated pairings, but a single intense experience can sometimes form a strong association, which may explain why one painful visit at nine still shapes his response at sixteen.\n\nThe conditioned response is now visible. The smell "makes his heart race and his hands sweat before any treatment has started": a conditioned fear response, triggered by the smell alone, with the same physical arousal that pain produces naturally. His heart rate rose from 72 to 108 beats per minute between home and the waiting room, a rise of 36 beats per minute, which shows that the setting alone can trigger the physical fear response.\n\nClassical conditioning therefore explains the fear as a learned link between the waiting room and pain: the smell, once neutral, now produces the fear response on its own.\n\n(b) [6]\n\nOperant conditioning explains behaviour through its consequences: a behaviour followed by reinforcement becomes more likely, and one followed by punishment becomes less likely. Tomás\'s cancellations fit this pattern. The scenario records their consequence: "each time he cancels he feels relieved". Cancelling removes the anxiety of the visit, so the relief is negative reinforcement, an unpleasant feeling taken away, which strengthens the cancelling. This explains why the avoidance has continued: he has cancelled his last two appointments, and each cancellation has been rewarded by relief.\n\nThe dentist\'s programme could change this by reinforcing attendance instead of avoidance. Its "short, painless visits" are small steps, a process called shaping, so Tomás is reinforced for each manageable approach to the dentist rather than being asked to face a full course of treatment at once. Each visit earns him a reward he has chosen, which is positive reinforcement; because he picked the reward, it is more likely to be something he values. Over repeated visits this reward can compete with the relief of cancelling, so attending gradually becomes the behaviour that is reinforced.\n\nOperant conditioning therefore explains the cancellations as avoidance kept going by negative reinforcement, and the programme could change them by making attendance, rather than cancelling, the behaviour that is rewarded.\n\nSources: Tomás scenario (supplied text); course notes on classical and operant conditioning (course notes)'

BI_P8D_Q = "I'm a Biology HL student (the course first assessed in 2025; my exams are in May 2027). Paper 2 Section A data-based practice question. Researchers at Lowmere University (a fictional university; practice data written for this exercise) randomly allocated 24 volunteers to two groups of 12. The ice-water group held one hand in water at 2 °C for 3 minutes; the warm-water group held one hand in water at 35 °C for 3 minutes. Blood samples and heart rate were taken before immersion and at the end of immersion. Ice-water group: mean plasma adrenaline concentration 0.30 nmol dm⁻³ before and 0.84 nmol dm⁻³ at the end of immersion; mean heart rate 68 beats min⁻¹ before and 85 beats min⁻¹ at the end of immersion. Warm-water group: mean plasma adrenaline concentration 0.32 nmol dm⁻³ before and 0.36 nmol dm⁻³ at the end of immersion; mean heart rate 70 beats min⁻¹ before and 71 beats min⁻¹ at the end of immersion. The researchers compared the increase in adrenaline concentration between the two groups with a t-test for two independent samples and obtained t = 5.41; the critical value at p = 0.05 is 2.07. A Psychology student will use your answer to evaluate a study of stress and memory, so state each result clearly with its units. (a) Calculate the percentage increase in mean plasma adrenaline concentration in each group. [2] (b) Calculate the percentage change in mean heart rate in each group, and the mean duration of one cardiac cycle, in seconds, in the ice-water group at the end of immersion. [3] (c) State the number of degrees of freedom for the t-test, and what the result shows at p = 0.05. [2] (d) Explain how adrenaline secreted by the adrenal glands prepares the body for vigorous activity, including the role of its receptor in the plasma membrane and of cyclic AMP. [3] (e) Suggest one reason why these data do not show that adrenaline alone caused the rise in heart rate in the ice-water group. [1] Write no more than 300 words, plus working."
BI_P8D = '(a) Ice-water group: (0.84 − 0.30) ÷ 0.30 × 100 = 180%, an increase of 180%. [1]\nWarm-water group: (0.36 − 0.32) ÷ 0.32 × 100 = 12.5%, an increase of 12.5%. [1]\n\n(b) Ice-water heart rate: (85 − 68) ÷ 68 × 100 = 25%, an increase of 25%. [1]\nWarm-water heart rate: (71 − 70) ÷ 70 × 100 = 1.4%, an increase of 1.4%. [1]\nIce-water cardiac cycle at the end of immersion: one cycle = 60 s ÷ 85 beats = 0.71 s. [1]\n\n(c) Degrees of freedom = n₁ + n₂ − 2 = 12 + 12 − 2 = 22 (no unit). [1]\nNull hypothesis: there is no difference between the groups in the increase in adrenaline. Since t = 5.41 is greater than the critical value 2.07, the null hypothesis is rejected: the difference between the groups is significant at p = 0.05. [1]\n\n(d) The adrenal glands secrete adrenaline into the blood, which carries it to target cells such as liver and muscle cells. [1]\nAdrenaline is hydrophilic and cannot cross the membrane, so it binds to a specific receptor in the plasma membrane; this activates an enzyme that converts ATP into cyclic AMP, a second messenger inside the cell. [1]\nCyclic AMP activates enzymes that break down glycogen into glucose. [1]\nThe glucose is respired in muscle cells, releasing energy that is transferred to ATP for vigorous activity. [1 spare]\n\n(e) Cold water also stimulates nerves that speed the heart, so the data do not separate the effect of adrenaline from that of nerve signals. [1]\n\nMark tags are provisional: they come from my own illustrative plan, not from an official markscheme.\nSources: your question and its data (supplied text); Biology course notes (course notes)'

